The standard library can perform the running addition directly. Starting with `0LL` makes the accumulation use `long long` instead of an `int` temporary. This is short, although a normal loop can be easier to step through while debugging.

An array with one value simply returns that value, and `n = 0` gives zero.

```cpp
#include <numeric>

class Solution{
public:
	int sum(int arr[], int n) {
		long long total = std::accumulate(arr, arr + n, 0LL);
		return static_cast<int>(total);
	}
};
```

`std::accumulate` visits each element once, so the time complexity is `O(n)` and the extra space is `O(1)`. The array remains unchanged.

This version adds the last included value and then asks for the sum of the part before it. When there are no values left, the answer is zero.

A small dry run for `[3, 5, 1]` is `sum(3) = 1 + sum(2)`, then `5 + 1`, and finally `3 + 6 = 9`.

```java
class Solution {
  public int sum(int arr[], int n) {
      return addFromEnd(arr, n - 1);
    }

  private int addFromEnd(int arr[], int index) {
      if (index < 0) {
          return 0;
      }
      long value = arr[index] + (long) addFromEnd(arr, index - 1);
      return (int) value;
  }
}
```

There are `n` recursive calls, so the time is `O(n)` and the call stack uses `O(n)` space. The base case also handles `n = 0`.

A single pass is enough here. Start at zero and add each of the first `n` values to the total. The input array is only read, not changed.

For example, with `[4, 7, 2]`, the running values are `4`, then `11`, then `13`.

```cpp
class Solution{
public:
	int sum(int arr[], int n) {
		long long total = 0;
		for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
			total += arr[i];
		}
		return static_cast<int>(total);
	}
};
```

The loop takes `O(n)` time and the extra space is `O(1)`. The final answer is returned as an `int`, matching the platform method.

Python already has a function that adds all values in a sequence, so there is no need to write the loop again. The method uses the first `n` elements, which also keeps the code aligned with the given parameter.

An empty array returns `0` naturally.

```python
class Solution:
	def sum(self,arr, n):
		return sum(arr[:n])
```

The operation takes `O(n)` time. Slicing creates a temporary list, so the extra space is `O(n)`.

The index moves from zero until it reaches `n`. A `long` total is used while adding, which avoids doing the intermediate additions in a smaller type. The input is not modified.

For `[2, 2, 6]`, the total changes from `0` to `2`, then `4`, and ends at `10`.

```csharp
class Solution
{
    public int sum(int[] arr, int n)
    {
        long total = 0;
        int index = 0;

        while (index < n)
        {
            total += arr[index];
            index++;
        }

        return (int)total;
    }
}
```

This uses `O(n)` time and `O(1)` extra space. As required by the method signature, the returned value must fit in an `int`.

